# Russian grammar -- manifest.
#
# Third language for the engine. Where Swedish stressed gender + definiteness,
# Russian stresses CASE: nominal endings fuse case and number, and the same
# machinery that handled Swedish pronoun case (nom/acc) now runs over full noun
# declensions. The article system disappears entirely (Russian has no articles),
# so this fragment is simpler at the top of the NP and much heavier in morphology.
#
# Syntax of this DSL is documented in en.gram; the same parser serves all three.
#
# Scope of this first slice
# -------------------------
#   * Nominative and accusative only (the other four cases -- genitive, dative,
#     instrumental, prepositional -- and adjective concord are later increments).
#   * Animacy: an animate masculine noun takes the genitive ending in the
#     accusative (кот -> кота); inanimate masculines and neuters leave the
#     accusative identical to the nominative (журнал, окно). See morph.gram.
#   * Full present-tense subject-verb agreement in person AND number (six endings:
#     читаю / читаешь / читает / читаем / читаете / читают), unlike English's lone
#     3sg contrast -- so agreement is two ordinary equations, no precomputed `vagr`.
#   * Zero copula: Russian has no present-tense 'to be', so predication is bare
#     subject + predicate-nominal ("он студент" = "he [is a] student").
#
# Known non-coverage (honest abstention, NOT claims of ungrammaticality)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * Free word order. Case-marking makes OVS/OSV grammatical in Russian; this CFG
#     fixes SVO, so a valid scrambled sentence parses as `no-analysis` rather than
#     being (wrongly) flagged. Stated up front so the abstentions are expected.
#   * Accusative plural (which re-introduces animacy across all genders) is out;
#     a plural object therefore abstains rather than risk a false diagnosis.
#   * Aspect (perfective/imperfective), past tense, and prepositions are unmodelled.
#
# Features
# --------
#   num    = sg | pl            number
#   pers   = 1 | 2 | 3          person (nouns are 3; pronouns carry their own)
#   gender = masc | fem | neu   grammatical gender (carried on nouns; concord later)
#   case   = nom | acc          grammatical case (this slice)
#   anim   = anim | inan        animacy (selects the masculine accusative form)
#   tense  = pres | past        finite tense (only pres is built out here)
#   vform  = bare | fin         verb form (infinitive vs finite)
#   valency= tr | intr          transitivity (whether a verb takes a direct object)

%feature num     : sg | pl
%feature pers    : 1 | 2 | 3
%feature gender  : masc | fem | neu
%feature case    : nom | acc
%feature anim    : anim | inan
%feature tense   : pres | past
%feature vform   : bare | fin
%feature valency : tr | intr
%feature lemma   : *

# Russian parses from S and splits on whitespace. No clitics and no contractions.
%start S
%tokenizer whitespace

%include closed.gram
%include morph.gram
%import  lexicon.tsv
%include syntax.gram
